## 3. Bounded Blocking Queue

A thread-safe queue with one twist: **it makes threads wait** instead of failing.

| Operation | Normal queue | Blocking queue |
|---|---|---|
| Take from empty | Returns null / throws | **Sleeps** until something arrives |
| Add to full | Fails / grows forever | **Sleeps** until space frees up |

C++ has no standard blocking queue, so you build one with `std::queue`, `std::mutex`, and two condition variables (one for "not empty," one for "not full"):

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <functional>

class TaskScheduler {
    std::queue<std::function<void()>> queue_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable not_empty_;
    std::condition_variable not_full_;
    static constexpr size_t MAX_SIZE = 1000;

public:
    void submitTask(std::function<void()> task) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        not_full_.wait(lock, [this] { return queue_.size() < MAX_SIZE; });
        queue_.push(std::move(task));
        not_empty_.notify_one();
    }

    void workerLoop() {
        while (true) {
            std::function<void()> task;
            {
                std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
                not_empty_.wait(lock, [this] { return !queue_.empty(); });
                task = std::move(queue_.front());
                queue_.pop();
                not_full_.notify_one();
            }
            task();   // run OUTSIDE the lock — critical!
        }
    }
};
```

**What you get for free:**

1. **Thread safety** — many producers + many consumers, no corruption
2. **Backpressure** — fast producers automatically block when consumers fall behind
3. **Efficient waiting** — idle threads sleep, no CPU burn

**Critical detail:** `task()` runs **outside** the lock. Holding the lock while running tasks would serialize all work and defeat the entire purpose.

**Sizing the queue:**

```
capacity = expected_throughput × seconds_of_burst_to_absorb
```

E.g., 100 tasks/sec workers, want to absorb 10s of spike → capacity = 1000.

