## 4. Three Strategies When the Queue Is Full

You must pick one based on use case:

| Strategy | C++ Method | Behavior | When to use |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Block** | `wait()` until space | Producer waits forever | Internal pipelines, batch jobs |
| **Timeout & reject** | `wait_for(timeout)` → throw | Wait briefly, then fail | **Request paths** — return 503 |
| **Drop immediately** | check size, return false | Fail instantly | Lossy events (analytics, metrics) |

### Timeout-and-reject pattern (for HTTP APIs):

```cpp
void enqueueOrReject(Task task) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    bool ok = not_full_.wait_for(lock, std::chrono::milliseconds(100),
        [this] { return queue_.size() < MAX_SIZE; });
    if (!ok) {
        throw std::runtime_error("503: Too many requests, try again");
    }
    queue_.push(std::move(task));
    not_empty_.notify_one();
}
```

> **Better to fail 5% of requests in 100ms than to fail 100% of requests in 30 seconds.**
> This is called **load shedding**.

