## 5. Graceful Shutdown

Workers blocked in `wait()` will sit there forever. You must wake them up cleanly.

**Why it matters:** Production systems restart constantly (deploys, autoscaling, pod evictions). Without graceful shutdown:
- In-flight tasks lost
- Database transactions left open
- Resources leaked
- Users see errors instead of clean responses

### Pattern 1: Atomic flag + `notify_all`

```cpp
class TaskScheduler {
    std::queue<std::function<void()>> queue_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable not_empty_;
    std::atomic<bool> running_{true};

public:
    void shutdown() {
        running_ = false;
        not_empty_.notify_all();   // wake every blocked worker
    }

    void workerLoop() {
        while (true) {
            std::function<void()> task;
            {
                std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
                not_empty_.wait(lock, [this] {
                    return !queue_.empty() || !running_;
                });
                if (!running_ && queue_.empty()) break;   // clean exit
                task = std::move(queue_.front());
                queue_.pop();
            }
            task();
        }
    }
};
```

Worker wakes up, sees `!running_`, drains remaining tasks, then exits.

### Pattern 2: Poison pill

Submit a sentinel "STOP" task per worker. Each worker exits when it sees one.

```cpp
struct StopSignal {};
using QueueItem = std::variant<std::function<void()>, StopSignal>;
// Worker checks std::holds_alternative<StopSignal>(item) and breaks.
```

Useful when you can't directly signal threads or want them to finish queued work first.

### Pattern 3: Poll with timeout

Worker waits up to N ms; if nothing arrives, checks shutdown flag. Trades latency for simplicity.

```cpp
not_empty_.wait_for(lock, std::chrono::seconds(1), [this] { return !queue_.empty(); });
if (!running_) break;
```

