## 6. The Actor Model

A fundamentally different approach: **don't share state at all. Send messages.**

An actor is just three things:

1. A **mailbox** (queue of incoming messages)
2. A **single-threaded message loop** that processes one message at a time
3. The ability to **send** messages to other actors

```cpp
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <thread>
#include <atomic>

template<typename T>
class Actor {
private:
    std::queue<T> mailbox_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
    std::atomic<bool> running_{true};
    std::thread thread_;

public:
    Actor() : thread_([this] { run(); }) {}

    void send(T message) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        mailbox_.push(std::move(message));
        cv_.notify_one();
    }

    void stop() {
        running_ = false;
        cv_.notify_one();
        thread_.join();
    }

protected:
    virtual void onReceive(T message) = 0;

private:
    void run() {
        while (running_) {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
            cv_.wait(lock, [this] { return !mailbox_.empty() || !running_; });
            if (!running_ && mailbox_.empty()) break;
            T msg = std::move(mailbox_.front());
            mailbox_.pop();
            lock.unlock();
            onReceive(std::move(msg));   // runs OUTSIDE the lock
        }
    }
};
```

**Why this is magical:**

Because `onReceive` only ever runs on **one thread at a time**, the actor's internal state never faces concurrent access. **You can have mutable fields, counters, caches — no locks needed in business logic.**

### Example: chat user as actor

```cpp
struct Message {
    enum Type { INCOMING, MARK_READ, GET_STATUS } type;
    std::string body;
};

class UserActor : public Actor<Message> {
    int unreadCount_ = 0;       // no lock needed
    std::vector<std::string> recent_;

protected:
    void onReceive(Message m) override {
        switch (m.type) {
            case Message::INCOMING:
                recent_.push_back(m.body);
                unreadCount_++;
                break;
            case Message::MARK_READ:
                unreadCount_ = 0;
                break;
            case Message::GET_STATUS:
                // ... reply to sender
                break;
        }
    }
};
```

No mutex. No race conditions. Synchronization happens only at the mailbox boundary.

