## 7. Actors vs Blocking Queues — When to Use Which

The decision rule is simple:

> **Does the work have a natural owner?**

| Situation | Use |
|---|---|
| "Process these tasks, any worker can handle any task" | **Blocking queue** |
| "I have many independent entities, each with its own state" | **Actors** |

| Examples |  |
|---|---|
| Resize image, send email, generate report | Queue (work has no owner) |
| Chat user, game room, IoT device, order book | Actor (entity owns its state) |

### Why actors win for stateful entities

With a blocking queue + shared map of users, **every operation contends on one big lock** (or you get tangled into per-user locks with deadlock risk). With actors, **Bob's actor and Carol's actor run truly in parallel** — no contention.

### Trade-offs of actors

| Lose | Gain |
|---|---|
| Direct synchronous calls (everything is async) | No locks in business logic |
| Easy debugging (must trace message flow) | Natural parallelism per entity |
| Strict global ordering | Easy distribution across machines |
| Simplicity for plain task processing | Centralized synchronization at one boundary |

