## 9. Real-World Pattern #1 — Async Background Work

**Disguise:** "This operation takes too long to do inline."

**Example:** User signs up. You need to save them (fast, 10ms) AND send a welcome email (slow, 500ms). Doing both inline = user stares at a spinner.

**Solution:**

```cpp
struct EmailTask {
    std::string recipient;
    std::string templateName;
};

class SignupService {
    BlockingQueue<EmailTask> emailQueue_;   // bounded, e.g. 10000
    UserRepository& repo_;

public:
    void signup(const std::string& email, const std::string& name) {
        repo_.save(email, name);                          // fast: save user
        emailQueue_.put({email, "welcome"});              // fast: enqueue
        // returns immediately — user sees instant response
    }

    void emailWorker() {
        while (true) {
            EmailTask task = emailQueue_.take();          // blocks if empty
            sendEmail(task);                              // slow, but no one's waiting
        }
    }
};
```

### Other examples in this disguise

| Problem | Fast (inline) | Slow (queued) |
|---|---|---|
| Image upload | Save raw bytes | Resize, compress, upload to S3 |
| Checkout | Save order | Charge card, email receipt, ship label |
| Report generation | Mark "pending" | Run query, generate CSV |
| User signup | Save user | Welcome email, analytics events |

### Interview soundbite

> *"I'll decouple the request path from background processing using a bounded blocking queue. The handler enqueues a task and returns immediately while workers process asynchronously."*

