## 10. Real-World Pattern #2 — Bursty Traffic & Load Shedding

**Disguise:** "Traffic comes in waves — most of the time it's fine, then suddenly it's 100x."

**Examples:**
- News site → quiet, then breaking news → 100x spike
- Ticket seller → silent, then concert tickets drop → meltdown
- Webhook receiver → 100/min, then upstream flash sale → 10,000/min

### The dumb solution: scale workers for the peak

Need 1000 workers for the spike? Run 1000 workers always. Pay for 1000 workers all year. They sit idle 99% of the time.

### The smart solution: queue absorbs the burst

- Size workers for **normal** load (e.g., 100)
- Size queue to **absorb the spike** (e.g., 100,000)
- During spike: queue fills, workers churn at sustainable rate
- After spike: queue drains, system returns to normal

```cpp
struct PurchaseRequest {
    std::string userId;
    std::string eventId;
    int quantity;
};

class TicketService {
    std::queue<PurchaseRequest> queue_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable not_empty_, not_full_;
    static constexpr size_t MAX_SIZE = 100000;   // absorbs ~10s at 10k/sec

public:
    // Producer — fails fast under overload
    void purchase(PurchaseRequest req) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        bool ok = not_full_.wait_for(lock, std::chrono::milliseconds(100),
            [this] { return queue_.size() < MAX_SIZE; });
        if (!ok) {
            throw std::runtime_error("503: Too many requests, try again");
        }
        queue_.push(std::move(req));
        not_empty_.notify_one();
    }

    // Worker — runs at steady rate
    void worker() {
        while (true) {
            PurchaseRequest req;
            {
                std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
                not_empty_.wait(lock, [this] { return !queue_.empty(); });
                req = std::move(queue_.front());
                queue_.pop();
                not_full_.notify_one();
            }
            processPurchase(req);
        }
    }
};
```

### Why this works

The spike is **time-shifted**, not eliminated:

- 10s spike at 10,000 req/s = 100,000 requests
- Workers process 100 req/s
- Drain time after spike: ~17 minutes

The work still gets done, just spread over a survivable window. Some users wait extra seconds, but **nothing crashes**.

### Interview soundbite

> *"I'll put a bounded queue between the API and workers. Workers run at sustainable capacity, the queue absorbs spikes, and producers timeout-and-reject if it overflows. This protects downstream systems and lets me size for normal load instead of peak."*

