## 12. Interview Cheat Sheet

### The mental model

| Concept | One-liner |
|---|---|
| Blocking queue | Thread-safe queue that **sleeps** instead of failing |
| Backpressure | Producers automatically slow down when consumers can't keep up |
| Load shedding | Reject some requests fast so the rest can be served |
| Graceful shutdown | Wake blocked threads cleanly so no work is lost |
| Actor | Entity with private state + mailbox; processes messages one at a time |
| Scheduler trick | Many actors, few threads, scheduler enforces "one thread per actor at a time" |

### When to reach for what

- *"Background work needed"* → **bounded blocking queue**
- *"Bursty traffic"* → **bounded blocking queue + `wait_for` timeout**
- *"Many independent stateful entities"* → **actor model**
- *"Just need work handoff between threads"* → **blocking queue** (don't over-engineer)

### One-line answers to drop in interviews

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| Background work? | *"Bounded blocking queue. Handler enqueues, returns immediately. Workers process async."* |
| Bursty traffic? | *"Queue absorbs the burst. Workers run at steady rate. Producers timeout-and-reject on overflow."* |
| Shutdown? | *"Atomic flag + `notify_all`, predicate checks the flag. Or poison pill if I can't signal directly."* |
| Stateful entities? | *"Actor model. Each entity owns its state, processes messages sequentially, no locks in business logic."* |
| Why not unbounded? | *"Unbounded queue removes backpressure. Memory grows until OOM."* |

### The universal rules

1. **Bound everything.** No unbounded queues. Ever.
2. **Backpressure always.** Slow producers > crashed consumers.
3. **Shutdown is a feature**, not an afterthought.
4. **Run tasks outside locks.** Locks guard data, not work.
5. **Predicates on every `wait()`.** Spurious wakeups are real.
6. **Pick the right tool.** Queue for tasks, actor for entities.

---

> The best concurrent code is code that **doesn't need to be concurrent inside.**
> Make each unit (worker or actor) sequential, and let the world be parallel between them.
